{
  "dom_id": "step-q280472",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The remainder of dividing $q(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-4x-5$ by $x-2$ is{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The remainder theorem states that when $q(x)$ is divided by $(x-2),$ the remainder equals $q(2).$ \n\nEvaluating $q(2)$ directly gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(2) & =2(2)^{3}+(2)^{2}-4(2)-5 \\\\  & =16+4-8-5 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $q(x)$ is divided by $(x-2),$ the remainder is $7.$\n\n**Note:** We can check the answer by carrying out synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-5$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n|  | $2$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
